    },
    {
      "id": "E5c7T-End4HKv-arSxhHQ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 330.4799118267301,
      "y": 503.21428814285724,
      "width": 751.2394409179688,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 876777476,
      "version": 1595,
      "versionNonce": 717657148,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793289256685,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An SM has four pools it hands out: thread seats, block seats, registers, and\nshared memory. Each block that lands takes some from every pool. \n\nHow many blocks fit = whichever pool runs out first. Check all four,\nthe smallest answer always wins.\n\nExample card: 32 block seats, 2,048 thread seats, 65,536 registers, max\n1,024 threads per block. \n\nTiny blocks waste seats: 32 threads per block -> 32 blocks fill the\nblock seats but only use 1,024 of the 2,048 thread seats. Half full. \n\nOdd sizes waste seats: 768 threads per block -> 2 blocks fit (1,536), \nthe 3rd won't. 512 thread seats sit empty. Three quarters full.\n\nRegisters per thread: 65,536 / 2,048 = 32 registers each if you \nwant it full. Use 64 each -> only 1,024 threads fit, whatever\nblock size you pick. Half full.\n\nDon't do this math by hand. Nsight Compute's occupancy calculator\ndoes it for your real card.\n\nSummary: an SM has fixed room for threads, blocks, registers,\nand shared memory. Your block takes a bit of each, and whichever\nruns out first sets how many blocks fit, and so how many warps\nare there to hide waiting. Very small blocks, awkward block sizes,\nand kernels with lots of local variables all leave\nthe SM partly empty. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An SM has four pools it hands out: thread seats, block seats, registers, and\nshared memory. Each block that lands takes some from every pool. \n\nHow many blocks fit = whichever pool runs out first. Check all four,\nthe smallest answer always wins.\n\nExample card: 32 block seats, 2,048 thread seats, 65,536 registers, max\n1,024 threads per block. \n\nTiny blocks waste seats: 32 threads per block -> 32 blocks fill the\nblock seats but only use 1,024 of the 2,048 thread seats. Half full. \n\nOdd sizes waste seats: 768 threads per block -> 2 blocks fit (1,536), \nthe 3rd won't. 512 thread seats sit empty. Three quarters full.\n\nRegisters per thread: 65,536 / 2,048 = 32 registers each if you \nwant it full. Use 64 each -> only 1,024 threads fit, whatever\nblock size you pick. Half full.\n\nDon't do this math by hand. Nsight Compute's occupancy calculator\ndoes it for your real card.\n\nSummary: an SM has fixed room for threads, blocks, registers,\nand shared memory. Your block takes a bit of each, and whichever\nruns out first sets how many blocks fit, and so how many warps\nare there to hide waiting. Very small blocks, awkward block sizes,\nand kernels with lots of local variables all leave\nthe SM partly empty. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UJYYDdE3IUhPwyFbtDCKW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 329.4403370345983,
      "y": -380.0000002857143,
      "width": 747.179443359375,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
